(ARG-10 — the Basic Usage example (LANGUAGE.md:4571-4578) compiles and
 behaves as the surrounding prose says: count into a number, `the argc`
 with its article, name into a text.
 One deviation from verbatim, and only one: the example's final
 `Print the program.` prints argv[0], which is whatever path the binary
 was invoked by and so cannot be re-checked by diff. It printed `./p`
 when run by hand with the command below; the probe keeps the
 declaration and prints a deterministic verdict about it instead.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox ARG-10.vox -o p && ./p alpha beta
 Args: alpha beta
 expected output:
   Argument count: 
   3
   Program name: 
   the program name is present
)
a number called argc is arguments's count.
Print "Argument count: ".
Print the argc.

a text called program is arguments's name.
Print "Program name: ".
If the program is empty then,
    Print "the program name is empty".
Otherwise,
    Print "the program name is present".
